j_1JKR5RQG81S0Y2CZ/pjd_1KVBDQ0TR1S00178/pdd.pdf\",\"t\":\"Project Design Document | 17 Jun 2026\"},{\"h\":\"/ghg-entry/rmv_1KQ7ASAEH1S0Y09K?tab=calculation-view\",\"t\":\"See GHG entry →\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J82BZ1QF1S02XC1/prj_1JKR5RQG81S0Y2CZ/pjd_1KVBDYF0D1S0NYD6/validation_and_verification_report.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J82BZ1QF1S02XC1/prj_1JKR5RQG81S0Y2CZ/pjd_1KVBDR2DA1S07AYJ/ghg_statement_report.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J82BZ1QF1S02XC1/prj_1JKR5RQG81S0Y2CZ/pjd_1KVBDQ0TR1S00178/pdd.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J82BZ1QF1S02XC1/prj_1JKR5RQG81S0Y2CZ/pjd_1KVB8837T1S0834W/project_name_change_record.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J82BZ1QF1S02XC1/prj_1JKR5RQG81S0Y2CZ/pjd_1K74RV7XF1S0T1R0/public_comment_period_summary.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"}],\"text\":\"VALIDATED\\nEW\\n1,000Y+\\nERW Deployment: Seoni & North Chhattisgarh, India\\nMati\\nISSUED\\n492.4\\nCREDITING PERIOD\\n23 Nov 2023 to 22 Nov 2033\\nSummary\\nRemoval method\\nCalculation overview\\nFeedstocks\\nProject documentation\\nCertificates\\nReversal risk\\nSummary\\n\\nThis project covers a subset of Mati Carbon’s enhanced weathering deployments in Central India, where Mati applies basalt powder sourced from the Deccan Traps large igneous province. Rice paddies experience extremely high water fluxes, which helps to expedite basalt dissolution and thus, CO2 drawdown. Mati works exclusively with smallholder farmers. Mati’s ERW deployments are agricultural interventions that act to improve productivity of cropland at smallholder farms. Mati’s ERW deployments provide outsized value to smallholder farmers who often cannot afford agricultural inputs such as fertilizer or pH balancing agents.\\n\\nPROJECT DETAILS\\nCurrent verifier\\nE\\nEarthood\\n->\\nProject code\\nZGXN\\nApproval date\\n17 Jun 2026\\nCurrent protocol version\\nEnhanced Weathering in Agriculture v1.2\\n⌘ + scroll to zoom\\nERW Deployment: Seoni & North Chhattisgarh, India\\nHow carbon is being removed\\n\\nEnhanced Weathering (EW) accelerates the Earth's natural chemical weathering process to remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere. In chemical weathering, carbon dioxide dissolved in rainwater reacts with minerals in rocks, partly dissolving them and producing stable bicarbonate ions. These bicarbonate ions are transported by rivers to the ocean, where they are safely stored for thousands of years. EW is typically carried out by spreading crushed reactive rocks or minerals on croplands.\\n\\nProject Design Document\\n17 Jun 2026\\nCalculation overview\\nCalculations demonstrating 283.32 tCO₂e removed from ERW Deployment: Seoni & North Chhattisgarh, India on 26 Jan 2026\\nSee GHG entry →\\nNet CO₂e Removed\\n283.323 tCO₂e\\nSequestrations\\n311.650 tCO₂e\\n-\\nActivities\\n28.327 tCO₂e\\n⌘ + scroll to zoom\\nView full screen\\nFeedstocks\\n\\nFeedstocks are the validated input materials that have been used for this project.\\n\\nName\\tCategory\\tValidated\\nN Chhattisgarh Basalt Dust\\tMineral & rock\\t26 Jun 2026\\nSeoni Basalt Dust\\tMineral & rock\\t26 Jun 2026\\nShowing 2 of 2\\nPrevious\\nNext\\nProject documentation\\nSubmission date\\tTitle\\tDownload\\n17 Jun 2026\\tValidation and Verification Report\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n17 Jun 2026\\tGHG Statement Report\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n17 Jun 2026\\tPDD\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n17 Jun 2026\\tProject Name Change Record\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n09 Oct 2025\\tPublic Comment Period Summary\\tPDF\\n->\\nShowing 5 of 5\\nPrevious\\nNext\\nCertificates\\nIssued\\n492.4\\nRetired\\n100\\nCrediting period\\n23 Nov 2023 to 22 Nov 2033\\nIssuances\\nRetirements\\nIssue date\\tIssued\\tSupplier allocation\\n17 Jun 2026\\t283.32\\t283.32\\n17 Jun 2026\\t209.08\\t209.08\\nShowing 2 of 2\\nPrevious\\nNext\\nReversal risk\\n\\nThe risk of reversal for this project has been assessed as no observable risk. No certificates from this project are allocated to a buffer pool.\\n\\nLearn more about reversal risk\"}"